    "text": "I'm working out my own rendering of this Old Babylonian complaint letter to Ea-nasir (UET 5, 81), deliberately avoiding the published translation. I've gone through the opening lines of the obverse: the greeting from Nanni, and Ea-nasir's earlier promise to deliver good ingots to Gimil-Sin. Now I'm turning to the lines that follow.\n\nNow I'm going through the next lines of the letter, where Ea-nasir sets out ingots he admits are not good and tells the messenger to take them or leave. I'm also working through Nanni's complaint about being treated with such contempt, and the damaged passage where I'm not sure of the reading.\n\nI'm checking the rest of the obverse and the start of the reverse, where Nanni says Ea-nasir sent his men away empty-handed more than once and asks which of the Dilmun traders has treated him this way, with the messenger's treatment noted at line 6.\n\nI'm weighing how to phrase Ea-nasir's rhetorical question about the silver he took from Nanni, settling on a natural rendering rather than the stock translation. Next I'm pulling in the copper accounting: eighteen talents given to the palace on Nanni's behalf, and another eighteen from Šumi-abum, set against what remains.\n\nI'm now going through the rest of the obverse: the messenger's silver for the palace and the temple of Šamaš, and the copper accounting around it. The next passage has Nanni asking how Ea-nasir can treat him this way over that copper, and accusing him of holding his purse in enemy territory, before he asks for the money to be returned in full.\n\nI'm now looking at the copper accounting and the closing demands in the letter: Nanni wants his purse restored in full, and he says he won't accept poor-quality copper from Ea-nasir from now on. The numbering then seems to jump from 26 toward the later lines, so I'm checking whether that gap is just a numbering slip in the source.\n\nI'm checking the cuneiform transliteration line by line, where the phrase about shaking out the ingots one by one in my own yard is continuous with what precedes it. I'm also weighing how the damaged left edge and the apparent numbering gap should be treated, deciding to read the text as continuous since the syntax carries straight through.\n\nI'm working through the damaged passage here: a clause about rejecting the copper because Ea-nasir has treated Nanni with contempt, and a line about the way he is being regarded that is too broken to read with confidence. I'll keep the rendering of the rejection cautious and note the ambiguity.\n\nI'm settling on the wording for the damaged phrase, keeping it bracketed since I'm not sure of the reading, and I'll render \"gubārī\" as copper ingots while I write out the letter.\n\n",
